1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:13,524
Your lab notebook lives in your own Obsidian vault. This is a day at the bench and how it gets into the notebook, by talking to Claude Code. The command behind each step is a footnote; you never type it.

2
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:23,351
In the morning you write the plan in Obsidian, in today's entry, as in a paper notebook: a line or two under a heading.

3
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:37,342
You photograph the gel with your phone and AirDrop it to the laptop. Drag the file into the Claude Code window and say: add this photo of gel 27 to today's notebook, lane 3 is the control.

4
00:00:37,342 --> 00:00:49,812
The original goes to the lab server, never overwritten; the vault keeps a thumbnail and the link; Claude Code adds one line on what it sees, marked as a reading, not the record.

5
00:00:50,199 --> 00:01:07,407
With gloves on you record a voice note: lane four has an extra band, re-run it tomorrow. Dropped into Claude Code the same way, it goes to the server and is transcribed when the speech-to-text key is set up. Here it was not, and the entry says so.

6
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:23,335
In Obsidian the day is one page: the plan, the gel with its thumbnail and the link to the original, the voice note. At the top, the project and the audience: who may read this entry.

7
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:44,533
Long prompts are easier to write in Obsidian than in a terminal. So you write them in a any note, select them, and press one key: the prompt is pasted into Claude Code and recorded with the time. A hook records every prompt that reaches Claude Code the same way, so none scrolls away and vanishes.

8
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:59,278
At the end of the day you ask the oracle what you noted about gel 27 and what is for tomorrow. It answers from the entry, block by block, with the times, and says what it could not read.

9
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:14,074
At the end of the week: make my report for my supervisor since Monday. It takes only the entries you marked for a supervisor, lists every entry and file it drew on, and writes a page, and sends nothing until you say so.

10
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:30,356
Day to day: photos and voice notes go from the phone to the laptop and into Claude Code with a sentence. Prompts are written in Obsidian and sent with a key. Sharing is per entry, by its audience.
